fix_sha: 0866332737090e613787765617611e95933c079d
fix_subject: fix(auth): rotate session secret on password change to evict other sessions
touched_paths (patch, product-file-scoped): triggarr/web/routes.py
planted_hunks: routes.py @@ -1445,17 +1445,7 @@ change_password (drops the session_secret rotation from the atomic config write); routes.py @@ -1469,26 +1459,11 @@ (drops the fresh-cookie re-issue under the new secret)

## What the reversed patch re-introduces
The fix (0866332) made `change_password` rotate `session_secret` in the SAME atomic
config write as the new password hash — every auth path (AuthMiddleware, login,
Basic auth) validates cookies against that secret, so all existing sessions are
evicted immediately, and the acting request is re-issued a fresh cookie under the
new secret so the password-changer stays logged in. The reversed patch (this
.patch = `git diff 0866332 0866332^ -- triggarr/web/routes.py`) removes both the
rotation and the re-issue: after a password change, every cookie signed with the
old (unchanged) secret stays valid for the full 30-day COOKIE_MAX_AGE — a stolen
or compromised session SURVIVES the password change, defeating the most common
reason users change a password (locking out an active intruder). Security-axis
session-eviction defect; subtle because the remaining code still "works" — the
password hash updates fine, only the eviction guarantee is silently gone.

